xnu 0096: keep the console log on the phone

The console only exists as pixels on the panel, and a boot writes several
screens of log: whatever the interesting line is -- a crash, the process
sampler, a driver's first error -- it is off the screen long before anybody
can photograph it. This is the same answer sweet has (its 0047), ported:
every character that reaches the console is also kept in a 4 MiB ring in RAM,
and a kernel thread rewrites the dirty blocks to the last 4 MiB + 4 KiB of
userdata once every 5 s, flush included.

The tee is in _cnputs(), so it catches kernel printf and the daemons' writes
to /dev/console alike (and it is before the mute check, so a quiet screen
still logs). Block 0 of the area is a header: "MONDLOG", u32 version, u32 ring
size, u64 total bytes written, u64 flush sequence; byte n of the stream is at
ring offset n % ring size. The area is behind the HFS image, which starts at
the partition's first block.

Read a previous boot by starting a dump kernel with boot-arg
mondriandump=1; it scans the ring and prints selected first/last matches before
stopping. The shim log viewer is unreliable on this UFS host. Boot-arg
mondrianlog=0 turns persistence off.

diff --git a/osfmk/console/serial_console.c b/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
--- a/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
+++ b/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
@@ -270,6 +270,16 @@
 _cnputs(char *c, int size, bool poll)
 {
 	extern int disableConsoleOutput;
+
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MONDRIAN)
+	/*
+	 * Before the mute check, and so before the panel: the persistent log in
+	 * bsd/dev/mondrian_ufs.c is what a boot is read from, the screen is
+	 * only the immediate view. It never blocks.
+	 */
+	extern void mondrian_log_append(const char *c, int size);
+	mondrian_log_append(c, size);
+#endif
 
 	if (disableConsoleOutput) {
 		return;
